\paragraph{RASP (recovery-aware composite).}
The recovery-aware score combines importance and functional overlap:
\begin{equation}
    S_\text{rasp}(h) = I(h) \times \big(1 - F(h)\big)^\alpha,
    \label{eq:score}
\end{equation}
where $\alpha \ge 0$ controls the strength of the overlap penalty ($\alpha{=}0$ reduces to Standard; our main comparison uses $\alpha{=}1.0$, which collapses under GRPO, while dose-response analysis identifies $\alpha{=}2.0$ as optimal; \S\ref{sec:sparsity_cliff}).
We choose the multiplicative form so that structures with zero importance are automatically pruned ($S{=}0$) regardless of overlap; an additive alternative $\beta I(h) + (1{-}\beta)(1{-}F(h))$ would retain zero-importance structures if their overlap is low.
Structures with high importance \emph{and} low overlap receive the highest scores and are retained; structures with high overlap are penalized and pruned preferentially.
At the empirically optimal $\alpha{=}2.0$, the diversity term $(1{-}F(h))^2$ dominates the score, making \rasp{} effectively CKA-guided with importance as a tiebreaker rather than a co-equal factor (\S\ref{sec:sparsity_cliff}).
However, \rasp{} $\alpha{=}2.0$ outperforms CKA-only by 2.2\,pp (66.11\% vs.\ 63.91\% SFT; Table~\ref{tab:alpha}), confirming that importance filtering provides complementary signal beyond diversity preservation alone.

